# ============================================================
# Host cron jobs (run as dr) - Telegram monitoring + sampling.
#
# The disk/heartbeat cleanup job runs as ROOT (deleting root-owned
# service dirs needs it) and lives in scripts/crontab.root.sample.
# Install:
#     crontab scripts/crontab.sample            # dr crontab (this file)
#     sudo crontab scripts/crontab.root.sample  # ROOT crontab (heartbeat)
# or merge the lines into each crontab with crontab -e.
#
# Each job handles its own logging/alerting, so cron output is
# sent to /dev/null. Adjust /home/dr/frigate if the host deploy
# dir differs.
#
# USER OWNERSHIP: every monitoring/report/sampler/restart job runs as dr (the
# deploy owner) - NOT the `ai` edit user. The `ai` crontab must be EMPTY.
# MIGRATION from the legacy `ai` crontab (2026-10-02):
#   1. install as dr:        crontab /home/dr/frigate/scripts/crontab.sample
#   2. empty the ai crontab:  sudo crontab -u ai -r
#   3. state/CSV files these scripts write are still owned by `ai`; dr cannot
#      write an ai-owned 664 file, so either chown (keeps history) or delete:
#        sudo chown dr:dr /home/dr/frigate/machine-monitor.state \
#          /home/dr/frigate/media/watchdog/watchdog_baseline_baseline_pre.csv \
#          /home/dr/frigate/media/watchdog/watchdog_baseline_baseline_pre.stat
#        # or: rm -f those files  (the CSV history is lost; dr recreates them)
# The ROOT heartbeat crontab is separate and must stay ROOT.
# ============================================================

# CPU temperature watchdog (python) - EVERY MINUTE (was */10)
# Two-tier Telegram alert (tunables in config/telegram.conf):
#   * CRITICAL: any single sample >= CRITICAL_TEMP (default 80 °C) alerts at once.
#   * WARM: MIN_HITS samples >= MAX_TEMP within MIN_WINDOW_MIN alert (default
#     72 °C, 3 in 240 min). History persists in machine-monitor.state.
# Every-minute sampling is REQUIRED: this host's hot spikes last ~1 min and a
# 10-min cadence missed the 83 °C peak (2026-09-08). If you edit this file's
# cadence, re-apply the whole crontab (crontab scripts/crontab.sample).
* * * * * /usr/bin/python3 /home/dr/frigate/scripts/machine-monitor.py >> /dev/null 2>&1

# Daily machine health report (python) - 08:00 every day
# (Telegram summary of host + Frigate state, per-camera online/offline)
0 8 * * * /usr/bin/python3 /home/dr/frigate/scripts/machine-status.py >> /dev/null 2>&1

# Machine+Frigate state watchdog (python) - every minute
# Cron mode (scripts/watchdog_baseline.py --cron): appends one sample per tick
# to a persistent, size-capped CSV (default <deploy>/media/watchdog/; falls back
# to ~/watchdog when the deploy dir isn't writable by the cron owner). Use a
# distinct --tag per comparison window - e.g. "baseline_pre" before a stream
# quality change, then "after_bump" once it's applied. CSV rows are bounded by
# --max-rows (default 20000).
* * * * * /usr/bin/python3 /home/dr/frigate/scripts/watchdog_baseline.py --cron --tag baseline_pre --out /home/dr/frigate/media/watchdog >> /dev/null 2>&1

# Per-camera offline watchdog (python) - every 2 minutes
# Alerts (Telegram) when a camera's Frigate camera_fps stays 0 for CAMERA_OFFLINE_HITS
# runs (default 3), and reports recovery after CAMERA_ONLINE_HITS (default 2).
# ALL cameras offline is flagged as a stack issue (Frigate/go2rtc/network), and a
# Frigate API outage is alerted too. Debounced, so the 04:00 Frigate restart and
# brief drops do not spam. See plans/camera-resilience-and-frigate-restart.md.
*/2 * * * * /usr/bin/python3 /home/dr/frigate/scripts/camera_monitor.py >> /dev/null 2>&1

# Daily Frigate restart (python) - 04:00
# Resets the CPython/THP memory sawtooth (measured 2.90 -> 1.56 GiB). Quiet on
# success; Telegram alert on failure. Set MAX_LOADAVG (env or telegram.conf) to
# skip when the host is busy. Remove this line once THP=madvise is persistent.
0 4 * * * /usr/bin/python3 /home/dr/frigate/scripts/restart_frigate.py >> /dev/null 2>&1
